BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td (Hemline Swap) Tj T* () Tj T* (HEMLINE SWAP: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY) Tj T* () Tj T* (Problem) Tj T* () Tj T* (College students face a recurring expense that makes little economic sense. Galas, sorority and) Tj T* (fraternity formals, career fairs, interviews, cultural events and graduation all demand) Tj T* (formalwear, and most students own little of it. They buy outfits for a single night, wear them) Tj T* (once or twice, and then store them. In a survey we ran at our university with 412 respondents,) Tj T* (the average student spent 118 dollars on formalwear in the past year and wore each piece an) Tj T* (average of 1.4 times. Sixty-one percent said they had bought something for an event that they) Tj T* (never wore again. For students on tight budgets, 118 dollars is a meaningful sum, and for) Tj T* (first-generation and lower-income students the pressure to look the part at an interview or a) Tj T* (formal can be a real barrier to participation. Existing options do not solve this well.) Tj T* (Commercial rental services are priced for adults with salaries, ship by mail with fixed windows) Tj T* (and sizing risk, and rarely cover the specific styles students want. Resale apps are built for) Tj T* (everyday fashion, not for borrowing a dress for Saturday.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Solution) Tj T* () Tj T* (Hemline Swap is a mobile marketplace built specifically for one-night formalwear among college) Tj T* (students. A student photographs an item, tags the size, style, occasion and condition, and) Tj T* (lists it in under two minutes. Other students on the same campus browse by event type, size and) Tj T* (date, and request a rental, a swap or a purchase. Because users are on the same campus, pickup) Tj T* (and return happen in person within a day, which removes shipping delays and fit uncertainty,) Tj T* (since students can ask a classmate how an item runs. Payment, a refundable damage deposit and a) Tj T* (rating system are handled in the app. Listing is free, so supply grows from the closets) Tj T* (students already have. We think the product is distinctive because it combines three things) Tj T* (competitors do not: hyperlocal supply, event-based search, and a student-only trust layer using) Tj T* (verified university accounts.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Market) Tj T* () Tj T* (There are roughly 19 million students enrolled in US colleges and universities. If we assume) Tj T* (that a typical student attends three to four formal events a year and spends between 100 and) Tj T* (150 dollars annually on formalwear, annual campus formalwear spending is in the range of 2) Tj T* (billion to 3 billion dollars. We treat this as our addressable market. Our initial serviceable) Tj T* (market is the set of mid-size and large residential campuses with active Greek life, formals) Tj T* (and career programs, which we estimate at about 600 institutions and 6 million students. If) Tj T* (Hemline Swap captured just five percent of that serviceable spending through rentals priced) Tj T* (well below purchase, annual transaction volume would exceed 50 million dollars. Our pilot) Tj T* (suggests demand is concentrated around predictable seasonal peaks, such as formal season in) Tj T* (autumn and spring and the interview season, which helps us plan inventory and marketing.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Business Model) Tj T* () Tj T* (Hemline Swap earns revenue three ways. First, we take a 15 percent service fee on every rental,) Tj T* (charged to the borrower on top of the lender price. Second, we take a 10 percent fee on resales) Tj T* (completed in the app. Third, we sell optional protection, a 2 dollar per rental damage waiver) Tj T* (that reduces deposit friction. Typical rentals in the pilot were priced at 15 to 35 dollars,) Tj T* (against a purchase price of 80 to 200 dollars for the same item, so borrowers save roughly 70) Tj T* (percent and lenders earn money on items that would otherwise sit unused. Our pilot average) Tj T* (order was 31 dollars of transaction volume, which implies about 4.65 dollars of fee revenue per) Tj T* (rental before protection add-ons. We expect a returning user to rent 3 to 4 times a year, and) Tj T* (since customer acquisition on campus is largely organic through events, student organizations) Tj T* (and word of mouth, we expect acquisition costs to remain low. Later revenue options include) Tj T* (sponsored listings from local tailors and dry cleaners, and a cleaning-and-return service that) Tj T* (students can add for a fee.) Tj T* () Tj ET
endstream
